3.text:
In the mean field the first two moments of the two linear filters are

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k} ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the white--noise approximation
$\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Big[\mu^{2}
+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}c_{kl}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Putting $w_{k}=w$ and $\mu_{k}=\mu$ and
$S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S$,
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
 =\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S+\phi w\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\Big[1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho w\mu S .
\end{equation*}
Hence, with $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}
=-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi}\,
\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\sigma^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\,{\displaystyle
-\,\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi}
\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\sigma^{2}}\,}\;S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual Poisson normalisation
$\sigma^{2}/(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\to\mu$ the time constants drop out
and, up to the inessential additive constant, one may write

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\propto\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the conventional sign convention
$\kappa=- \rho/\phi\mu$ up to the additive $1$.  In particular the
limiting value $S\to\infty$ is $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ and the
conventionally quoted dimensionless offset is

\[
\kappa_{{\rm off}}\equiv\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\]

which is often written in the literature in the sign convention
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  With the normalisation $\sigma^{2}=
\mu(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$ this is the $\kappa$ which appears in
$c^{*}=[\kappa S-1]/[S-1]$, i.e. $\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$
up to the usual $+1$ additive convention.  Putting the additive $1$
into the definition this is the familiar form
$\kappa\propto-\rho/\phi\mu$, the $1+\rho/\phi\mu$ form quoted above
being the one most often used. ∎

2.text:
In the quasi static limit $v_{k}\simeq \phi x_{k}$ and
$u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ the mean drift of the $k$-th
weight is

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
   =\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
   =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big\langle x_{k}x_{l}\big\rangle
     +\rho w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big\langle x_{k}x_{l}\big\rangle
      +\rho\mu S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put 
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl}$ with $c_{kk}=1$ and
$c_{kl}=c$ for $k\ne l$.  Then

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
      =\mu^{2}\Big[\,s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big]
      =\mu^{2}\Big[\,1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the uniform state $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\displaystyle\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual notation $c^{*}=( \kappa S-1)/(S-1)$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
\end{equation*}
is the  (normalised) depression–potentiation ratio.  In the
convention in which the self–correlation is kept explicit the
self–term can be absorbed in $\kappa$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi\,\sigma_{x}^{2}}
      \;\;\xrightarrow{\;\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu\;}\;
      \kappa=-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi}
      =-\,1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which for the normalised rate $\mu\equiv1$ is the familiar
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi$, i.e. up to the inessential $-1$ the
depression–potentiation ratio $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  With the
low–pass filters kept explicit $\sigma_{x}^{2}\to\sigma_{x}^{2}
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})^{-1}$ etc. and the same ratio is obtained as
$\kappa=-(\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\sigma_{x}^{2}$,
which with the Poisson normalisation $\sigma_{x}^{2}=\mu$ reduces to
$\kappa=-(\phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi$, i.e. to the
normalised form $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above.  This is the
usual $\rho/\phi\mu$ depression–potentiation factor which sets the
asymptotic critical correlation $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.
In particular with the rate normalised to $\mu=1$ the form most often
quoted is $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi$, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi$, the
minus sign expressing the fact that $\rho<0$ is a depressive offset.
∎

