3.text:
In the homogeneous mean field put

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\frac{\phi}{\tau_{v}}\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}
x_{k}(t-s)ds ,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\frac1{\tau_{u}}\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-s/\tau_{u}}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
so that for a stationary process with $ \langle x_{k}\rangle=\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle
 =\mu^{2}+\sigma^{2}c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$, $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle=
\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\mu^{2}
+\phi w\,\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
     =\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
gives on the uniform state $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}S+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho\mu S=0,
\qquad S\equiv S_{k},
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
\frac{\phi\mu^{2}+\rho\mu}{\phi}\,
\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\sigma^{2}}\,S .
\end{equation*}
With the Poisson white–noise normalisation 
$\sigma^{2}/(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\to\mu$ the filter overlap drops out
and
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{\rho S/\phi\mu+1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the signed offset–to–Hebbian drive ratio.  Absorbing the explicit
self term $s_{kk}c_{kk}=1$ into the definition of the constant one may
write the critical correlation in the conventional form
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential additive $+1$ and the overall sign convention
$\dot w_{k}\propto \pm u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ the dimensionless number which
controls the $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$ is the
depression–potentiation ratio
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\infty}\propto\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
normally quoted in the depressive convention $\rho<0$ as the positive
number $\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  In particular with the
rate normalised $\mu\equiv1$ this is the familiar
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi$, $c^{*}\to1+\rho/\phi$ for $S\to\infty$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi>0$.  Keeping the filter overlap explicit the
combination which actually appears is
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu$, i.e. in the
un-normalised form
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=-
\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi}\,
\frac{\mu(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})}{\sigma^{2}}
\;\xrightarrow{\;\sigma^{2}=\mu(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\;}
-\,\mu-\frac{\rho}{\phi},
\end{equation*}
which with the sign convention $\dot w_{k}\propto -u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
is quoted as $\kappa=(-\rho/\phi+\mu)(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})$.
Removing the inessential $+1$ and the overall sign this is the
text-book $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, the heterosynaptic offset
normalised by the Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$, which is the $S\to\infty$
limit of $c^{*}$.  With the $+1$ kept explicit this is the
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above, $\kappa\to-\rho/\phi\mu$ for
$|\rho|\gg\phi\mu$.  The $-1$ in the numerator is the self interaction
$s_{kk}c_{kk}=1$. ∎

