5.text:
In the mean field $w_{l}\equiv w$, $\mu_{l}\equiv\mu$ put on the $w$ time
scale

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}\ ,\qquad 
u_{k}\simeq w\sum_{l}s_{kl}x_{l}\ ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu$ and $\langle x_{k}x_{l}
\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big]
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[1+c\big(S-1\big)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
 =\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho w\mu S ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
The zero of the mean weight therefore satisfies

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=-
\frac{\,1+\displaystyle\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S\,}{S-1}
      =\frac{-\displaystyle\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the self interaction $s_{kk}c_{kk}=1$ kept explicit the
critical correlation can be written in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}:=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
for the conventional depressive offset $\rho<0$.  In the $S\to\infty$
limit $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ and the balance is $\phi\mu+\rho=0$.
Absorbing the trivial self term $s_{kk}c_{kk}=1$ in the definition of
the dimensionless offset to Hebbian drive this is usually quoted in the
self–absorbed form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that with the depressive sign $\rho=-\theta<0$

\begin{equation*}
\kappa =1-\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\theta}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the rate normalisation
$\mu\equiv1$ this is the familiar $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi$, $\kappa_{0}
=-\rho/\phi$, the $-1$ in the numerator being the $s_{kk}c_{kk}=1$ self
term.  Up to the inessential additive $+1$ this is the
depression–potentiation, or offset-to-drive, ratio $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ which
sets the asymptotic threshold $c^{*}\to|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  Keeping the
low-pass overlap explicit,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}\mu^{2}
   +\phi w\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and on the Poisson normalisation
$\sigma^{2}/(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\to\mu$ the above $\kappa$ is recovered.
In un-normalised form the $S$-coefficient is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}= \Big(-\frac{\rho}{\phi}+\mu\Big)
\bigl(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}\bigr),
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{\rm eff}/(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})=-\rho/\phi+\mu$, which on the
usual $\mu$-normalisation reduces to $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$,
$\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, quoted above.  This is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$
offset-to-Hebbian-drive ratio that controls the $S\to\infty$ limit
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$. ∎

